The Open/Closed Principle (OCP) is the second SOLID principle.
It states: “Software entities (classes, modules, functions) should be open for extension, but closed for modification.”
This means you should be able to add new functionality by adding new code, not by changing existing code.
Achieve this by using abstraction (interfaces, abstract classes) and polymorphism.


Violation:
1. Show how the current implementation violates the Open/Closed Principle (OCP) and is incomplete.
2. Demonstrate the problem: Each payment type needs different data (e.g., card number, pin, bank account).
3. Every time a new payment type or new data is needed, you must modify the processPayment method, making the code hard to maintain and extend.

    // Java
    public class PaymentProcessor {
        public void processPayment(String type, double amount, String cardNo, String pin, String bankAccount) {
            if ("creditcard".equalsIgnoreCase(type)) {
                if (cardNo == null || pin == null) {
                    throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                        "Card number and PIN required for credit card payment");
                }
                System.out.println("Processing credit card payment of $" + amount +
                                " with card: " + cardNo + " and pin: " + pin);
                // Credit card logic
            } else if ("paypal".equalsIgnoreCase(type)) {
                System.out.println("Processing PayPal payment of $" + amount);
                // PayPal logic
            } else if ("bank".equalsIgnoreCase(type)) {
                if (bankAccount == null) {
                    throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                        "Bank account required for bank transfer");
                }
                System.out.println("Processing bank transfer of $" + amount +
                                " from account: " + bankAccount);
                // Bank transfer logic
            } else {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown payment type: " + type);
            }
        }
    }


Problem with above class:
1. The method signature grows as you add more payment types and their required data.
2. You must keep adding parameters and if-else logic, making the code error-prone and violating OCP.
3. Adding a new payment method or changing requirements for an existing one requires modifying this class, risking bugs in existing logic.

OCP Solution:
1. Use a PaymentMethod interface and separate classes for each payment type.
2. Each class handles its own required data and logic.
3. The processor uses the interface, so new payment types can be added without modifying existing code.


    // Java
    public interface PaymentMethod {
        void pay(double amount);
    }

    // Java
    public class CreditCardPayment implements PaymentMethod {
        @Override
        public void pay(double amount) {
            System.out.println("Paid $" + amount + " using Credit Card.");
        }
    }

    // Java
    public class PayPalPayment implements PaymentMethod {
        @Override
        public void pay(double amount) {
            System.out.println("Paid $" + amount + " using PayPal.");
        }
    }

    // Java
    public class PaymentProcessor {
        public void processPayment(PaymentMethod method, double amount) {
            method.pay(amount);
        }
    }

    // Java
    public class Main {
        public static void main(String[] args) 
        {
            PaymentProcessor processor = new PaymentProcessor();
            PaymentMethod creditCard = new CreditCardPayment();
            PaymentMethod payPal = new PayPalPayment();
            processor.processPayment(creditCard, 100.0);
            processor.processPayment(payPal, 200.0);
            // To add a new payment method, just implement PaymentMethod and use it here.
        }
    }

Summary:
You can add new payment methods by creating new classes that implement PaymentMethod, without changing PaymentProcessor or existing payment classes. This follows the Open/Closed Principle.

